<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    PALE STONE - what the tower is made of.

    THE WALLS OF A CARVED FLOOR ARE NATURAL ROCK. GenStep_DungeonLayout does not
    build walls; the map is pre-filled with rock and the layout SUBTRACTS rooms
    and corridors from it, so whatever rock the map generated with IS the wall
    shell. Making the tower white therefore means changing the rock, not adding
    a wall def.

    Which rock a map gets comes from RockNoises.Init, which reads
    Find.World.NaturalRockTypesIn(tile) - the tile geology, not the map
    generator. A pocket map inherits the tower tile, so it would otherwise get
    whatever granite or sandstone happens to be local, and the inside of the
    tower would change depending on where it was raised.
    GenStep_TowerWhiteout overrides that list.

    mineablePreventNaturalRockOnSurface IS THE CONTAINMENT.
    ThingDef.IsNonResourceNaturalRock requires that flag to be FALSE, and World
    builds allNaturalRockDefs from defs that pass it - so setting it true keeps
    pale stone out of every ordinary colony map geology while leaving it
    perfectly usable when a gen step names it directly.

    naturalTerrain AND leaveTerrain are both set on purpose:
    TerrainDefGenerator_Stone.ImpliedTerrainDefs skips generating the three
    implied stone terrains when both are present. Without that we would get an
    auto-generated PaleStone_Rough over vanilla grey RoughStone - grey-noisy
    ground under white walls.

    The texture is baked, not tinted. <color> is a MULTIPLY in the shader, so
    tinting vanilla grey Rock_Atlas white returns exactly the grey you started
    with. See Tools/make_pale_stone.py.
  -->
  <ThingDef ParentName="RockBase">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_PaleStone</defName>
    <label>pale stone</label>
    <description>The tower is made of this all the way up: a close-grained white stone with faint cool veining and no seams anywhere. It is not quarried and it is not laid. Wherever you cut into a floor, there is more of it, and it is the same.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Linked/PaleStone_Atlas</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <color>(255,255,255)</color>
      <linkType>CornerFiller</linkType>
      <linkFlags>
        <li>Rock</li>
        <li>MapEdge</li>
      </linkFlags>
    </graphicData>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>600</MaxHitPoints>
      <Beauty>0</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <building>
      <isNaturalRock>true</isNaturalRock>
      <isResourceRock>false</isResourceRock>
      <!-- Containment: keeps pale stone out of every colony map geology. -->
      <mineablePreventNaturalRockOnSurface>true</mineablePreventNaturalRockOnSurface>
      <mineablePreventMeteorite>true</mineablePreventMeteorite>
      <!-- Both set, so no grey implied terrains are auto-generated. -->
      <naturalTerrain>IsekaiDungeons_SanctumStone</naturalTerrain>
      <leaveTerrain>IsekaiDungeons_SanctumStone</leaveTerrain>
    </building>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
